---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const repo = 'https://github.com/binjesoft-ui/datalle';
---
<Base title="How it is built">
  <h1>How it is built</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The pipeline runs on Databricks Free Edition. The public site is a static Astro build on Vercel that reads
    small JSON exports of the gold tables, so it keeps working even if the Databricks workspace is idle.
  </p>

  <pre>NYC TLC parquet files (monthly)
        ↓
Bronze  raw data in Delta, only types normalised, lineage columns added
        ↓
Silver  validated rows  +  quarantine table (rows + failed rule names)
        ↓
Gold    hourly demand · daily trend · top zones · tip % · quality report
        ↓  export to JSON
Static site (this page) on Vercel</pre>

  <h2>Design decisions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Idempotent loads.</strong> Each month is written with <code>replaceWhere</code>, so re-running never duplicates data.</li>
    <li><strong>Quarantine, not delete.</strong> Every rejected row is kept with the rules it failed.</li>
    <li><strong>Rules as data.</strong> Quality rules are a dictionary of SQL expressions, covered by unit tests.</li>
    <li><strong>Null means fail.</strong> A missing value never passes a rule by accident.</li>
    <li><strong>Schema drift handled.</strong> TLC files change column casing and types between months, so bronze casts to one schema.</li>
    <li><strong>Tips are card-only.</strong> Cash tips are not recorded in the data, so tip % uses card payments only.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The medallion layers</h2>
  <p>
    Bronze keeps the raw data so any cleaning bug can be fixed and replayed without downloading anything again.
    Silver holds the validated rows and a quarantine table, which also makes every number traceable: raw rows =
    clean rows + quarantined rows. Gold holds small tables shaped for one question each, and those are the only
    tables the website reads. Storage is cheap, so keeping several copies costs little; the real cost is the extra
    processing and code to maintain.
  </p>

  <h2>Is Spark necessary here?</h2>
  <p>
    Honestly, no. One month of yellow taxi data is about 3 million rows, and a full year is roughly 35 to 40 million.
    That fits on a single modern computer, and a single-machine engine such as DuckDB or Polars can clean it quickly.
    Spark has startup and coordination overhead that only pays off when data no longer fits on one machine or when many
    jobs and users share the same data.
  </p>
  <p>
    I used Spark and Delta Lake on purpose: to practise the pipeline design (layers, idempotent loads, quarantine,
    tests) with the tools that scale to terabytes, using the same code at any size. Knowing when the heavier tool is
    unnecessary is part of the lesson.
  </p>

  <h2>Spark vs DuckDB: a planned comparison</h2>
  <p>
    The next step is to run the same cleaning rules in DuckDB on one machine and compare the two on the same files.
    Results will be published here once measured; no numbers are shown until then.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Same input:</strong> identical monthly TLC parquet files and identical quality rules.</li>
    <li><strong>Same output check:</strong> clean and quarantined row counts must match exactly, otherwise the comparison is not valid.</li>
    <li><strong>What gets measured:</strong> wall-clock time for 1 month, 1 year and as many years as the free tier allows, plus the hardware used.</li>
    <li><strong>What it should show:</strong> where a single machine is faster, and the size at which Spark starts to make sense.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Code</h2>
  <p>Everything is open source: <a href={repo} rel="noopener">{repo}</a></p>
</Base>
